 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 6,
   "local": {"cy": "Castell Harlech"},
   "text": "HARLECH CASTLE. Built 1283 to be unbeatable. Owain Glyndŵr beat it anyway. 'Men of Harlech' is the song about a siege here."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "HARLECH. Castle up the crag, beach down the hill, tea in between. Croeso, welcome."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "DOLPHIN WATCH. Look out to sea, count to twenty, then tell everyone you saw one. Bottlenose, nine times out of ten."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "BARMOUTH BRIDGE (Pont y Bermo). Wooden, 1867, nearly half a mile. Walkers pay a small toll. Seagulls travel free, as ever."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "BARMOUTH (Abermaw). Chips, 99s and a steep staircase of regret."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "THE HERMIT'S COVE. Deep water, no ferry. Needs a bit of wade."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "CADAIR IDRIS (893 m). Legend: sleep on it and wake up a poet or mad. Take a flask instead."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 47,
   "text": "BORTH. Six thousand year old tree stumps lie under this beach and show at low tide. They do not do tours."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 51,
   "text": "ABERYSTWYTH 14 miles. Keep going south. Left is the sea."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "cambrian_coast_dinas_oleu",
   "title": "Dinas Oleu",
   "text": "Dinas Oleu, the gorse covered hill above Barmouth, was the very first property of the National Trust. Fanny Talbot gave it in 1895 so it would stay wild, and it has managed that very well. The name means 'fortress of light', and on a good evening the view earns it."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 49,
   "plaque": "cambrian_coast_ospreys",
   "title": "Dyfi Ospreys",
   "text": "Ospreys came back to nest in Wales this century, and the marshes up the Dyfi estuary from Borth now have their own famous nest, watched by cameras. The birds winter in West Africa and return each spring to dive feet first for fish. In Welsh: gwalch y pysgod, the fish hawk."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "arts_hedd_wyn",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Hedd Wyn",
   "text": "Ellis Humphrey Evans, a shepherd from Yr Ysgwrn near Trawsfynydd, wrote poetry under the bardic name Hedd Wyn, 'blessed peace'. He was killed at Pilckem Ridge on 31 July 1917, the first day of Passchendaele. Six weeks later, at the National Eisteddfod in Birkenhead, his awdl 'Yr Arwr' won the Chair; the name was called three times, nobody stood, and the chair was draped in black. Y Gadair Ddu, the Black Chair, carved by a Belgian refugee, still stands in the farmhouse. The farm is open to visitors. Bring a coat and something to wipe your eyes with."
  }
 ],
